{
  "name": "Gmail Organiser",
  "nodes": [
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "## Organize your Gmail inbox automatically by sender  \nBy Tinashe Kwande\n\n## How it works\n\nThis workflow automatically organizes your Gmail inbox by applying sender-based labels to emails.\n\nOn a scheduled interval, the workflow checks your inbox for the most recent email that has already been marked as read. This ensures unread emails are never modified before you review them.\n\nOnce a read email is found, the workflow extracts the sender’s name and retrieves all existing Gmail labels. It then checks whether a user-created label already exists with the same name as the sender.\n\n- If a matching label exists, the workflow applies that label to the email.\n- If no matching label exists, a new label is created using the sender’s name and then applied to the email.\n\nAdditional checks ensure that only user-created labels are used and that the correct label is applied to the correct message. After a label is successfully applied, the email is removed from the Inbox, keeping your inbox clean while preserving access through labels.\n\nThis process runs continuously and safely, ensuring emails from the same sender are always grouped together.\n\n## Setup steps\n\n1. Import this workflow into your n8n instance.\n2. Connect your Gmail account credentials to all Gmail nodes.\n3. Adjust the Schedule Trigger to your preferred interval.\n4. Mark emails as read to allow them to be processed.\n5. Activate the workflow.\n",
        "height": 688,
        "width": 800
      },
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "typeVersion": 1,
      "position": [
        -2448,
        144
      ],
      "id": "74fc9aee-e414-40e8-9b87-9f6eaa97f8ba",
      "name": "Sticky Note1"
    },
    {
      "parameters": {
        "content": "## | Getting the latest read email and checking existing labels\n\nThis section retrieves the most recent email that has been marked as read, fetches all existing Gmail labels, and loops through them to find a label that matches the email sender.\n ",
